[w4-classify] Collection log page's `sections` map is the group taxonomy itself: H2s "Bosses"(3)/"Raids"(60)/"Clues"(64)/"Minigames"(76)/"Other"(99) each own a contiguous run of H3 sub-category rows (item titles, 2-space-indented in the sections listing) up to the next H2 -- no separate DPL table needed, the TOC IS the list. `get --section N --strip` on a group H2 index returns the full nested subtree (e.g. s3 Bosses = 29509B) not just that header's own bytes (11B in the map), so one get per group (5 total) suffices for citation instead of one per item. Non-group trailing sections (Combat stats, Ranks, Duplicate entries, Changes, Trivia) are page meta, not sub-categories -- skip. 123 sub-category rows total: bosses=56, raids=3, clues=11, minigames=22, other=31. Two title collisions vs the earlier minigame-domain census are legitimate, not duplicates: Fishing Trawler/Vale Totems/etc. appear in both classify:minigame:* and classify:clog:* namespaces because the collection-log's "Bosses" grouping intentionally includes activity-style entries (Tempoross, Wintertodt, The Gauntlet, The Inferno, Fortis Colosseum, The Fight Caves) that the minigame census also classified separately -- clog parent reflects the collection-log UI's own grouping, not a canonical boss/minigame taxonomy. "Magic Training Arena" is the literal header text on the Collection log page's own TOC (collection-log tab is titled slightly differently there than the "Mage Training Arena" article title used by the minigame census) -- kept verbatim per own-words-from-source rule rather than silently correcting to match the other domain.
[minigames-key] Minigame/D&D pages rarely have a single "Requirements" H2 that states everything -- entry-gating skill levels sometimes only live in the lead paragraph (get --section 0), not any numbered section (e.g. Pest Control's combat-level gate for Novice/Intermediate/Veteran landers, Volcanic Mine's Mining 50 to enter). Always check section 0 (the infobox+lead) if the sections list has no "Requirements" header before assuming the activity has no level gate.
[minigames-key] Reward tables that use {{StoreLine}}/{{DropsLineReward}} templates are readable as-is with --strip (item name, cost/rarity fields inline) -- no need to resolve the template, the raw params carry the values already.
[minigames-key] Several of these pages (Wintertodt, GotR, NMZ, Volcanic Mine, Blast Furnace) transclude their full reward table via {{PageName/Rewards}} or {{Main|X}} rather than inlining it in the Rewards section -- the fetched section text will just be a bare template call with no rows; if exact drop-table rows are needed downstream, fetch the linked subpage directly (e.g. "Wintertodt/Rewards") rather than re-fetching the parent section.
[unlocks-key] "Unlockable content" (page, not category) plus targeted per-item pages already had most of the fetch work done by prior passes (classify:unlock:*, travel:ref-fairy-ring/spirit-tree, w3-agi:*, questdb:client-of-kourend + the-depths-of-despair/the-queen-of-thieves/the-forsaken-tower/the-ascent-of-arceuus) -- 6 of 12 targets (Fairy ring, Spirit tree, Graceful outfit, Ardougne cloak, Xeric's talisman via CoX row, Kharedst's memoirs via the 5 Kourend quest rows) needed zero or near-zero new fetches, only synthesis into the unlockdb: schema. Always grep contrib.jsonl by target name FIRST (not just by the unlockdb: prefix) -- the shallow classify:unlock:* rows and various travel:/questdb:/w3-agi: rows carry real gate/grant facts under a different key namespace and schema, and skipping that cross-check would have re-fetched pages needlessly.
[unlocks-key] Achievement Diary's per-tier aggregate "All requirements" table (Achievement_Diary.s2, same <tabber> structure as the per-region DiarySkillStats tables already logged under [depth:01]) lists "Acquire a prospector helmet from the Motherlode Mine" as an Additional-requirement checkbox for BOTH the Hard AND Elite tier bundles across every region -- a genuine cross-dependency between two named steer-point targets in this same batch (Achievement Diary system, Prospector kit) that would be easy to miss fetching only the per-region diary pages individually, since it lives on the system-level aggregate page instead.
[unlocks-2] Several jewellery/equipment unlocks are diary-tier rewards, not craftable items with a skill/quest/item recipe: "Explorer's ring" and "Rada's blessing" have EMPTY `sections` output (zero numbered H2s) because the base title is a disambiguation stub listing 4 tiered variants (Explorer's ring 1-4, Rada's blessing 1-4) -- the real content lives on the numbered variant pages and on the dab page's own section-0 lead paragraph (get --section 0 --strip works fine even with zero sections listed). For these, gate.quests/items should name "<Diary name>, tier N" rather than a quest chain, since the true gate is Achievement Diary tier completion (tracked separately if a diarydb: row exists), not a recipe.
[unlocks-2] classify:unlock:* rows (from the [w4-classify]/[unlocks-key] "Unlockable content" page pass) exist for a handful of this batch's targets (camulet, ectophial, fire-cape, infernal-cape, ring-of-dueling-colosseum-teleport, void-knight-equipment, elite-void-knight-equipment) with a one-line "accelerates" hypothesis and a single shared ref (Unlockable_content.s4/s18) -- useful as a hint of what to verify but NOT sufficient as a contribute-ready unlockdb: row (no gate.skills/quests/items breakdown, no anchor_hint); still had to fetch each item's own page section 0 (and sometimes Creation/Teleportation-options sections) to get real gate/grant facts. Grep contrib.jsonl by bare item name (not just the unlockdb:/classify: prefixes) before starting, per the established [unlocks-key] convention, but expect to still do a full per-item fetch pass even when a classify:unlock: hint row exists.
[minigames-2] "Shades of Mort'ton" the quest and "Shades of Mort'ton (minigame)" the repeatable minigame are two separate wiki pages sharing a near-identical title -- `sections "Shades of Mort'ton"` returns the QUEST's TOC (Details/Walkthrough/Rewards), not the minigame's (Requirements/Gameplay/Loar Shades/.../Uniques); for minigamedb: deep-dives always try the base title first to confirm via its Otheruses hatnote whether a "(minigame)" sibling exists, then fetch sections on THAT title.
[minigames-2] "Trawler" and "Fishing Trawler" are the same target under two different names in a batch brief (confirmed via the wiki's own {{Redirect|Trawler|...}} hatnote pointing distinct meanings, but "Fishing Trawler" is unambiguously the minigame) -- treated as one contribution (minigamedb:fishing-trawler) and the duplicate as a free skip rather than two separate fetch passes.
[minigames-2] Reward-table wikitext for several of these minigames (Trouble Brewing's Honest Jimmy shop, Castle Wars' ticket exchange, Rat Pits) uses {{StoreLine|buy=N/A}} rows with no GE price column at all since the currency is a minigame-specific token (pieces of eight, Castle wars ticket) -- these are genuinely non-GE by design, not a stripped/missing field, so citing them item-name-only (no coin value) in contribute rows was correct rather than a gap to backfill.
[minigames-3] Raid pages (CoX/ToB/ToA) are inconsistent about inlining their own unique-drop tables: ToB and ToA's Rewards H2 lists item names directly even though it also carries {{Main|...}} to a dedicated chest page, but CoX's Rewards H2 (Chambers_of_Xeric.s29) is a bare {{Main|Ancient chest}} pointer with zero item names in the fetched text -- had to separately `sections`+`get --section 2 --strip "Ancient chest"` to get CoX's real unique table (Twisted bow/Kodai insignia/Elder maul/Dragon claws/etc with rarity weights), rather than assuming the parent raid page's Rewards section is self-sufficient like its two siblings.
[minigames-3] "The Gauntlet" is the correct page title for BOTH the regular Gauntlet AND the Corrupted Gauntlet variant -- there is no separate "Corrupted Gauntlet" content page (search surfaces one, but it's a Speed-Runner/Speed-Chaser leaderboard page, not an article); Corrupted Gauntlet content lives entirely under sections 11-13/16-17 (H2 "Corrupted Gauntlet" and its "Boss Room"/"Rewards" H3s, plus a shared "Loot table" H2 with separate Normal/Corrupted Unique Rewards H3 subsections) of The_Gauntlet's own section map. Contributed as two separate minigamedb: rows (gauntlet, corrupted-gauntlet) sharing one ref page, since the brief listed them as two targets despite being one wiki article.
[minigames-3] Several D&D/minigame Infobox Activity leads state an explicit numeric level gate inline in prose rather than a structured field: Zalcano's lead paragraph (section 0) states "Zalcano requires a minimum of 70 Mining and 70 Smithing to be fought" with no Requirements H2 or infobox field carrying it -- same [minigames-key] gotcha pattern (check section 0 first) but this is the first case where the gate is genuinely combat-adjacent-but-not-combat (need the levels to deal any damage at all, not just to survive better).
[gear-3] {{Drop sources|X}} and {{Store locations list|X}} (the standard "Item sources"/"Shop locations" H2 content on item pages) are Lua/DPL-driven templates that render as a BARE unexpanded template call through `get --strip` -- zero usable drop-monster/shop-NPC data comes back (confirmed across Dragon scimitar, Dragon boots, Twisted bow, Scythe of vitur, Abyssal whip). The item's own lead paragraph states the source when it bothers to (most do: "dropped by X", "purchased from Y") but some don't (Dragon boots' lead only says "cannot be made using Smithing", no source monster at all). When the lead is silent, try the monster's own page instead -- its "Drops > Weapons and armour" subsection uses {{DropsLine}} rows, which DO render correctly (confirmed on King black dragon, Mithril dragon, Brutal black dragon, Rare drop table) -- though this requires already suspecting a candidate monster to check, since there is no working reverse-lookup from item to source page.
[gear-3] Monster infobox `slaylvl` field is the authoritative "can this be damaged without X Slayer" gate and is worth checking even when the ITEM page's own lead already states a level (e.g. Abyssal whip's lead says "70 Attack to wield" but never mentions the 85 Slayer needed to actually damage its abyssal-demon source -- that fact only surfaces on Abyssal demon's own infobox/lead). For gear-unlock rows, fetch the drop-source monster's own page section 0 in addition to the item's, don't assume the item page states the full farming gate.
[gear-3] Raid unique-drop items (Twisted bow from Chambers of Xeric, Scythe of vitur from Theatre of Blood) have NO skill/quest gate on the unique roll itself beyond the raid's own entry bar -- CoX has literally no stated requirement at all (just Combat + a wide skilling-points spread that affects unique-roll odds, not eligibility), ToB's ONLY stated hard requirement is Priest in Peril (for Morytania access). Don't invent a level requirement for raid-unique gear rows; the gate is "can you complete/carry the raid," which is a much softer, point-scaled thing than a flat level check.
[normalize-arch] steps_quests.jsonl has NO code consumer besides the plan-*.mjs additive merge, and every row's location.region is "global" -- any region-based partition of quests must fuzzy-join quests.jsonl league_regions by slug (20/189 miss the exact join: quest-dt-style abbreviations, h-a-m punctuation slugs, -ii-the-fallen-empire suffixes; alnum-normalized containment matching recovers most, remainder -> a misc band).
[normalize-arch] A per-skill prefix grep over train-* rows silently misses 3 melee-adjacent odd ids (train-att-str-stronghold, train-mm-tunnels, train-nmz) that don't follow the train-<skill>-<band> pattern -- histogram the id prefixes before partitioning fan-out scopes by pattern.
[normalize-arch] gen_reference_catalog.py DROPS quest_db's already-structured start{npc,location} and length fields on the way to catalog.jsonl -- part of the "prose card" problem is a lossy builder, not missing research; check what the source row already holds structured before scoping any re-research of a derived surface.
[normalize-arch] synth-* step ids exist only in step_refs.jsonl (route-emission sidecar), not as steps.jsonl rows -- any sidecar keyed by step_id (train_methods.jsonl) must tolerate ids that are not steps.jsonl rows, and consolidators must not treat "id not in steps.jsonl" as an error.
[normalize-r] quest_db.jsonl diary rows conflate non-quest gates into two different places: the "quests" array literally mixes in non-quest strings (e.g. desert-hard's "Defeat the Kalphite Queen", kandarin-hard's "Access to Barbarian Fishing..."), while other rows (ardougne-elite, desert-elite, falador-elite/hard, fremennik-province-elite, kandarin-elite) instead carry a separate top-level "other" array holding the same class of gate (boss kills, guild access, partial-quest starts) that is absent from "quests" entirely -- card-facts normalization had to check BOTH fields per row before deciding a gate was "already structured" (drop) vs a genuine gap needing a required-for/boss fact; a naive drop-if-quests-nonempty rule would have silently lost ~15 real gates across the elite/hard tiers.
[normalize-r] Row-level "length" field is unreliable even where present: ardougne-easy's length is literally "??" despite its own notes text stating "10 easy tasks" in the first sentence -- the real count had to be read out of notes prose, not the structured field, and the structured-field gap flagged with its own caveat fact rather than silently overwritten.
[normalize-r] Across all 20 rows the reward-xp pattern is fully mechanical and internally consistent: easy=2,500xp/skill 30+, medium=7,500xp/40+, hard=15,000xp/50+, elite=50,000xp/70+ -- elite rows always state this directly in rewards.xp, but every easy/medium/hard row has rewards.xp="??" with the real number instead embedded as an "Antique lamp worth N xp in any skill >=M" line inside rewards.unlocks[]. Future diary-row normalization can rely on this tier->xp/threshold mapping to recover the value from unlocks without it being a fabrication (it's literal held text, just misfiled).
[normalize-r] card-facts FIX rows: quest_db.jsonl diary rows split "quests" arrays that mix real quest titles with non-quest achievement conditions ("Partial completion of X", "Defeat a Y", "Reach N% favour", "Own a Jagex Account") in the same array -- real titles get dropped per the already-structured-field rule, but the achievement-style entries need their own required-for facts since nothing else carries them forward.
[normalize-r] card-facts FIX rows: the 4 easy-tier diary rows per region (karamja/kourend/lumbridge/morytania/varrock-easy) hold only a generic boilerplate notes string, not a per-task breakdown -- do not fabricate per-task facts for these, emit an overview + caveat fact instead and move on.
[normalize-r] card-facts FIX rows: karamja-easy's rewards.unlocks antique-lamp text (1,000xp/any level) contradicts the row's own generic notes boilerplate (claims >=30 lamp) -- a genuine held-data self-contradiction, not a normalizer error; flagged via caveat fact rather than silently picking one.
[normalize-r] card-facts FIX rows: several diary rows' "other"/mixed-quests achievement conditions duplicate a task already spelled out in notes (e.g. kourend-and-kebos-elite/hard "Defeat a Lizardman Shaman", morytania-medium "In Aid of the Myreque" started) -- skipped the separate required-for fact in favor of the single task-level fact to avoid restating the same requirement twice.
[normalize-r] card-facts label enum (overview|boss|kills|...|caveat) has no slot for reqs.skills/reqs.quests -- by design those stay in quest_db.jsonl's structured reqs, do not restate as facts; only unstructured extras (reqs.other, per-task quest-gate nuance, item tool notes) are new signal worth a fact.
[normalize-r] "Wanted!" quest id contains a literal '!' -- safe to keep verbatim in card_id/key/JSON, but strip it for the output filename (row_Wanted.json) to avoid shell glob surprises; wikicli contribute --file handled the JSON content fine either way.
[normalize-r] steps_quests.jsonl ids use slugified quest names (lowercase, hyphens, apostrophes/exclamation marks dropped, e.g. quest-wanted for "Wanted!"); a plain grep for the slug is reliable and found all 12/12 quest rows in this batch.
[normalize-r] questdb-04's items-shaped reqs.items array sometimes hides a non-item string mixed in (Biohazard's "ability to fight a level 13 Mourner", Black Knights' Fortress' separate top-level quests_note holding a 12-QP+evasion gate) -- these were routed to combat facts instead of forced into req_items, same mixed-content pattern the diary rows already showed under prior [normalize-r] entries but now confirmed on quest rows too.
[normalize-r] Compound reqs.items strings joined with '+' for a single sub-task (Barbarian Training's six per-leg item bundles, e.g. "hammer + 2 matching metal bars + 2 matching logs (smithing leg)") don't reduce to one clean qty -- treated qty as "??" and put the leg name in note rather than fragmenting into per-material req_items entries, since the source never itemizes counts per component beyond what's already inline.
[normalize-r] rewards.xp appears in THREE different shapes across quest_db.jsonl quest rows: a flat dict ({"Skill": n}, e.g. Animal Magnetism), a plain string-array with no caveats (e.g. At First Light ["Hunter 4,500", ...], Beneath Cursed Sands ["Agility 50,000"]), and a prose string with an embedded condition (A Tail of Two Cats' "2x 2,500xp antique lamps (any skill 30+)"). Only the third shape needed an xp-note fact -- the first two are already clean structured values with nothing to decompose, so duplicating them as facts would violate the drop-if-already-structured rule.
[normalize-r] card-facts FIX rows (questdb-06 batch): 3 of 20 quest ids (druidic-ritual, fairytale-i-growing-pains, family-crest) have NO steps_quests.jsonl row at all under the expected quest-<slug> id -- confirmed via direct grep, not a slug-mismatch (fairytale-i is not filed under "quest-fairytale-1" either, despite that being the id fairytale-ii's own reqs.quests array references); steps_quests_id correctly set to null for these three rather than guessed.
[normalize-r] card-facts FIX rows (questdb-06 batch): reqs.items entries that are actually combat/mechanic gates rather than physical items recur beyond the earlier-logged pattern -- Enakhra's Lament's "Enough Prayer for ~5 seconds of Protect from Melee" and "Standard spellbook required" were routed to combat/mechanics facts, not forced into req_items, extending the [normalize-r] questdb-04 Biohazard/Black Knights' Fortress precedent to a third quest.
[normalize-r] card-facts FIX rows on quest_db.jsonl quest rows: "required for X"/"gates X" statements are commonly buried as plain strings inside the already-structured rewards.unlocks array (e.g. cabin-fever's "required for The Great Brain Robbery") -- the schema has no dedicated forward-gate field, so these are worth pulling into their own required-for fact despite technically duplicating an unlocks array entry; this is re-categorization into the typed slot the schema provides, not the pure-duplication the drop rule targets.
[normalize-r] card-facts FIX rows: an xp-note fact is warranted when a reward-xp value (in rewards.xp OR buried in rewards.unlocks) carries a real player CHOICE or level-gate CONDITION (e.g. "2x7,000xp, choose from Attack/Strength/.../Magic", "any skill 50+") or when rewards.xp is empty/"??" and the real number only exists in unlocks/notes prose (data-recovery case, per the earlier diary-tier antique-lamp gotcha) -- but a flat "N xp lamp from NPC" already-populated rewards.xp with no choice/condition is fully captured by the structured field and should NOT get a duplicate xp-note fact (confirmed by contrast: client-of-kourend/curse-of-the-empty-lord/desert-treasure-ii got xp-note facts for choice-bearing lamps, defender-of-varrock's plain "5,000xp lamp" did not).
[normalize-r] card-facts FIX tasks (no wiki fetch) benefit from splitting req_items quantity parsing strictly on the reqs.items string vs. carrying quantities mentioned only in prose notes as separate items-note facts -- keeps req_items mechanical/deterministic and avoids silently smuggling numbers from a different held field.
[normalize-r] quest_db.jsonl rows for lost-city and monkey-madness-i have no matching id in steps_quests.jsonl (grep for quest-lost-city / quest-monkey-madness-i returns nothing) -- steps_quests_id must be null for these two even though they're mainline quests; every other quest in the r-band (jungle-potion..monks-friend) resolved fine.
[backend-arch] quest-helper's DirectionArrow.java lives at src/main/java/com/questhelper/steps/overlay/DirectionArrow.java NOT steps/tools/ (only QuestPerspective/DefinedPoint are in steps/tools) -- and GitHub code search returns 0 hits for "DirectionArrow" in Zoinkwiz/quest-helper (search index unreliable for that repo); probe raw.githubusercontent.com paths directly instead of trusting code search.
[backend-arch] overlay-bridge PROTOCOL.md body still says the service listens on 127.0.0.1:43594 but the deployed truth (plugin-thin-client memory addendum 4) is host 43599 multi-bound 127.0.0.1,10.66.0.1 -- 43594 is the OSRS game port and collides with the proto test server; any doc extending the protocol must carry the 43599 port discipline or a reader will re-create the collision.
[backend-arch] run_scenario.py's injection surface is env-var-shaped (SPAWN_REGION/PLAYER_XY/WORLD_NPCS/SEND_PLAYER_INFO) and only those four exist today -- WORLD_OBJECTS/INVENTORY/VARBITS/INTERFACES are NOT implemented in serve.py despite SCENARIO_FORMAT.md's world block declaring objects/inventory/varbits fields; a design that replays UIs must budget the proto-server packet lanes (LOC add, container update, VARP 56/95, IF_OPENSUB) rather than assume the scenario format's world block is fully wired.
[normalize-r] card-facts row for a card_kind=quest not present in steps_quests.jsonl (nature-spirit, priest-in-peril) is legitimate -- both are older, well-established quests simply absent from that particular data file; steps_quests_id: null is correct, not a bug.
[normalize-r] questdb rows for RFD's 10 sub-chapters carry per-chapter reqs/rewards plus a whole-chain "totals" row (id recipe-for-disaster) whose own reqs.items list is meta-commentary ("see the 10 subquest entries"), not real gatherables -- route those into facts (items-note/combat), not req_items.
[normalize-r] royal-trouble and rum-deal rewards.xp is a free-text string, not a {skill:xp} dict, in this shard of quest_db.jsonl -- carry the string content into an xp-note fact rather than trying to parse it as a mapping.
[normalize-r] steps_quests.jsonl id lookup (quest-<slug>) misses ~half of RFD sub-chapters and rum-deal entirely -- treat absence as steps_quests_id:null, do not infer.
[normalize-r] questdb rows this shard (scorpion-catcher..temple-of-ikov) mix reqs.items strings that are pure items ("Chisel"), item-OR-skill alternatives ("Dusty key ... or Agility 70 ... to bypass shortcut"), and skill-only strings misfiled under reqs.skills as prose ("43 Quest Points (hard gate)", "Prayer 31 (boostable: no)") -- the skill-string ones never became req_items (they're not gatherable items) but their boostable/hard-gate qualifier is worth preserving as a caveat/overview fact rather than silently dropped.
[normalize-r] steps_quests.jsonl id lookup (quest-<slug>) missed exactly 4 of this shard's 20 (shadow-of-the-storm, swan-song, tai-bwo-wannai-trio, tale-of-the-righteous) with no discernible pattern (mix of Medium/Short length, some with long reqs.quests chains, some without) -- confirms [normalize-r]'s prior note that absence is common and must map to steps_quests_id:null, not be treated as a lookup bug.
[normalize-r] card-facts req_items: strings mixing a bundle ("4 oak planks, hammer, saw (only if building your own crafting table)") don't map cleanly to one name+qty — split the stated count into the note and leave qty "??" rather than force a single number onto a multi-item string.
[normalize-r] card-facts FIX rows (questdb-14, witchs-house/witchs-potion/x-marks-the-spot/zogre-flesh-eaters): witchs-house's reqs.skills array holds a pure combat-encounter string ("Ability to defeat 4 monsters (levels 19-53) back-to-back with no break") with no skill name/level at all, not just a mixed skill+prose case like earlier r-band rows -- routed entirely to a combat fact, zero skill-level content survives into reqs at all for this quest. zogre-flesh-eaters' rewards.unlocks mixes genuine forward unlocks (Relicym's balm recipe, comp ogre bow/brutal arrow fletching, inoculation bracelets, Uglug's shop access) with a one-time item reward (ourg/zogre bones, dropped) and an explicit "Bonus:" advice line about optionally selling the black prism for gold (dropped per the advice/meta-commentary rule) -- the "Bonus:" prefix is a reliable textual signal for advice-not-objective in this corpus.
[normalize-r] card-facts fact-decomposition: reqs.skills is sometimes a dict ({skill:level}) and sometimes a list of strings with inline reqs (e.g. troubled-tortugans, while-guthix-sleeps) — neither shape has a reqs.items key when the quest has no consumable requirements (troubled-tortugans, while-guthix-sleeps), so req_items correctly comes out empty; don't assume every quest_db row has an items array.
[normalize-r] card-facts req_items qty discipline: literal "quantities only when the string states them" means single nouns without a leading digit (e.g. "Beer", "A boat", "Any pickaxe") get qty:"??" even though real-world play implies 1 — resist the urge to infer 1, it's not "stated".
[normalize-r] quest_db.jsonl notes fields already end many rows with an explicit "Required for: X, Y, Z." clause — these map cleanly to the required-for fact label and are the highest-value extraction since that dependency info exists nowhere else as structured data in the row.
[normalize-r] card-facts FIX rows (norm-r-contrib-01, minigamedb/unlockdb bundle): the card-facts schema has no dedicated slot for reqs.skills/gate.skills (unlike req_items for reqs.items) -- same as the earlier quest_db.jsonl finding, plain skill-level requirements on minigame/unlock rows stay implicit in the held source row and are NOT restated as facts; only unstructured prose beyond the plain level number would warrant one.
[normalize-r] card-facts FIX rows: reqs.items strings on minigame rows sometimes hide a non-item recommendation mixed with a real item in the same string (Hallowed Sepulchre's "recommended: Hitpoints 70+, a Saradomin-affiliated item to use the Magical Obelisks without draining run energy") -- split into a caveat fact for the skill-level recommendation and a req_items entry (optional:true) for the actual item, rather than forcing the whole string into one req_items row.
[normalize-r] card-facts summary field's <=160 char cap is tight for minigame/unlock rows carrying multi-clause held facts (numbers + gates + locations) -- budget for at least 2 trim passes per row when drafting summaries that try to pack a number AND a gate AND a location into one sentence.
[normalize-r] card-facts schema has no location field -- minigame rows with a reqs.location.name/coords were folded into an 'overview' or 'start' fact instead of dropped, since coordinates are objective/checkable and otherwise lost.
[normalize-r] req_items is a poor fit when reqs.items is a restriction/exclusion list (e.g. Castle Wars 'no food/capes/cannon allowed', Soul Wars Nomad-tutorial ban list) rather than a genuine required/recommended item -- left req_items empty for those and captured the restriction as a 'start' fact instead of force-fitting name/qty.
[normalize-r] card-facts FIX rows (norm-r-contrib-03, unlockdb/minigamedb bundle): explorer's-ring/rada's-blessing gate.items strings describe a diary-tier requirement ("Lumbridge & Draynor Diary, tier matching the ring number"), not a gatherable item -- routed to a caveat fact and req_items left empty for the tier itself, only the trivial gp cost of extra copies (200-9,500gp) got a req_items entry; extends the norm-r-contrib-01 diary-tier precedent (kharedsts-memoirs) to two more unlock rows.
[normalize-r] card-facts req_items decomposition: several minigamedb rows bundle multiple physical items into one reqs.items string joined by commas/parens (e.g. shades-of-mortton's "Hammer (...), building materials (...), a flamtaer bag..., a tinderbox and appropriate-tier pyre logs + shade remains + sacred oil..., and a key of the matching metal/colour...") -- split each named component into its own req_items entry with qty "??" unless a count is explicitly stated, rather than collapsing a multi-item sentence into one row.
[normalize-r] card-facts summary cap (<=160 chars) needed 2-3 trim passes on 4 of this batch's 20 rows (ectophial, book-of-the-dead, rada's-blessing, shades-of-mortton) -- all four were unlock/minigame rows trying to pack a mechanism + a gate + a payoff into one sentence; reconfirms the prior norm-r-contrib-01 gotcha about budgeting extra trims for dense minigame/unlock summaries.
[normalize-r] card-facts FIX rows (norm-r-contrib-05, unlockdb spellbook bundle): unlockdb rows sometimes have gate.skills/gate.quests/gate.items ALL empty and the notes prose says so explicitly (arceuus-spellbook: "zero skill/quest/item gate") -- this genuine no-requirement state got a 'start' fact ("speak to Tyss...to swap in immediately") rather than being silently skipped, since the access mechanism itself (NPC/location/alt-routes) is unstructured signal worth keeping even when there's no gate to report.
[normalize-r] card-facts FIX rows (norm-r-contrib-05): a gate.quests entry sometimes carries an inline KB cross-reference comment appended to the quest name (lunar-spellbook: "Lunar Diplomacy -- see questdb:lunar-diplomacy in this KB for its full prerequisite chain") -- per the established reqs.quests-stays-implicit rule, this produced no fact (the cross-reference is meta-pointer, not new player-facing content), consistent with gotcha #31's skill-level precedent extended to quest-gate fields.
[normalize-r] card-facts FIX rows (norm-r-contrib-05): a secondary skill-gated unlock buried in grants/notes prose (arceuus-spellbook: "80 Construction lets a player build a dark altar in their POH...to swap spellbooks without traveling") is a genuine extra unlock layered on top of the card's own zero-gate base state -- captured as its own 'unlock' fact rather than folded into the 'start' fact, since it describes a different unlock path (Construction skill) than the base access method (walk to Tyss).
[normalize-r] card-facts FIX task on already-held minigamedb/unlockdb prose worked cleanly with zero wiki fetches -- every objective sentence in reqs/gate/rewards/grants/notes decomposed into a labeled fact 1:1 with no ambiguity, since the upstream contrib rows were already well-structured prose (not raw wikitext); the closed label enum (overview|boss|kills|combat|start|length|difficulty|unlock|required-for|hazard|mechanics|xp-note|items-note|removed|caveat) covered every statement type seen across 6 minigame + 14 unlock rows without needing an escape hatch.
[normalize-r] gate.items/reqs.items entries that are prose sentences (e.g. "a bucket (obtainable on-site) or Ice gloves to lift moulded weapons") need manual splitting into multiple req_items rows (one per named item) plus an optional:true + note when the sentence itself frames it as an either/or or "recommended" -- a single regex/split-on-comma pass would wrongly merge "metal bars, or metal equipment worth at least 2 bars" into two req_items when it's one alternative-item requirement; read each items[] string as a unit before deciding how many req_items rows it yields.
