## norm-q-misc-08 retro
Scope: 5 quests (Knight Waves, Learning the Ropes, Lost Lover, Merlin's Crystal, Natural history quiz), a small-content/quiz/tutorial batch. 69 atoms total (2+38+2+21+6) across 25 checkpoints, all 5 contributed fresh -- zero pre-existing questatoms:* keys at session start (verified against contrib.jsonl's 104-key inventory before any fetch).
Fetch cost: 5 `sections` probes (3 real 404s: Knight Waves/Lost Lover/Natural history quiz quick guides; 2 hits: Learning the Ropes/Merlin's Crystal) + 7 `get --section --strip` calls (2 fresh Knight Waves sections, 1 fresh Learning the Ropes parent section, 1 cache-hit Lost Lover, 1 fresh Merlin's Crystal parent section, 2 fresh Natural History Quiz sections) + 1 cache-hit whole-page Natural History Quiz reuse to confirm East/South/West room composition without a new fetch -- well under the 5-get-per-quest budget on every quest.
Coords: zero {{Map}}/{{NPC map}} templates found in any of the 5 quests' fetched text -- a fetch-confirmed gap across the whole batch, not a shortfall.
A local python validator (idx contiguity, checkpoint start_idx resolution + monotonicity, verb/target/cmp enum membership, 80-char label cap, non-empty refs, hint-type sanity) ran clean on first pass (0 errors, 69 atoms) before any contribute call, per the og-g5/norm-q-misc-05 precedent (gotchas #99/#120) -- worth continuing to script-generate+validate rather than hand-type JSON for any quest with >15 atoms (Learning the Ropes here).
Canonical trigger for next wave: when a held quest_db row makes a specific factual claim about the wiki's own page structure (e.g. "no separate Quick guide exists"), still run the `sections` probe on the flagged title yourself before trusting it outright -- it was correct here (Learning the Ropes), but the norm-q-kandarin-05 precedent already showed 3/3 false "verify exists" flags in the opposite direction, so neither direction should be trusted without a direct check.
[normalize-q] norm-q-misc-07: Hopespear's Will has NO separate /Quick_guide page -- `sections "Hopespear's Will/Quick guide"` cache-hits and resolves title to plain "Hopespear's Will" with the main page's URL/sha1, i.e. it silently redirects; treat as the main-page-fallback trap even though the get succeeds without error (no 404, no byte-identical-warning, just a quietly rewritten title/url in the fetch result -- must check the recorded title, not just fetch success).
[normalize-q] norm-q-misc-07: Enter the Abyss/Quick guide and Holy Grail/Quick guide are both flat (Details+single Walkthrough, or Walkthrough+3-4 H3s) -- small miniquest/short-quest Quick guides rarely nest as deep as the longer quests; budget 1-4 gets even before checking sections output.
[normalize-q] norm-q-misc-07: self-inflicted bug caught by validate pass -- when generating produces{}/consumes{} item-slug keys programmatically from an NPC-name list (e.g. "<name>s-bone" for 5 goblin priests), a hand-typed mirror dict for the final consuming atom silently drifted (redeyes/strongbones already end in "s", got double-s'd to "redeyess-bone"/"strongboness-bone"). Always derive the consuming dict from the same produced keys (or diff produced-vs-consumed sets) rather than retyping bone/item-slug names by hand across atoms.
[oppgran] og-g10 GENERATE (travel-atom pass): the wave-1 classify pass flags grain_class travel-implicit purely on prose pattern-matching -- it does not check whether the step already carries a complete atom{}. ori-t-07-walk-to-survival-expert (the ONLY travel-implicit row across all 205 classified rows s1-s4) already had a fully-formed atom{verb:walk-to, target:survival_expert} in steps_origin.jsonl before this pass ran. Extends og-g3's "check the step's own atom key before assuming the queue's step_ids list is authoritative" precedent to the travel/classify track specifically: a travel-implicit flag means "unwound prose mentions movement", not "atom is missing" -- always check the live steps file for an existing atom{} before treating a travel-implicit target as unatomized.
