[media-capture] step_media.jsonl's "sha6" field is NOT a content hash of the final gallery PNG/GIF bytes (checked two existing native rows against `sha1sum` of their own gallery-out file — neither matched even a truncated prefix) — it's a per-capture-event mint, decoupled from file content. A new wiring pass should mint its own fresh 6-hex ids (e.g. sha1 of a "<step_id>:<beat>" seed, salted on collision) rather than trying to derive one from the actual image bytes.
[media-capture] osrs-wiki's own assets/data/cache/{objects,npcs,items}.pack are a custom binary "OSRP@" format (header @4=count, 12-byte {id,offset,length} index, JSON body per record) — spike/tools/pack_search.py already implements a substring-over-name reader for objects.pack; generalizing it with a --path flag covers npcs.pack/items.pack too. locs/<region>.json.gz and npc-spawns/<region>.json.gz rows are `[id, type, rot, localX, localY]` and `[id, localX, localY]` respectively (confirmed against assets/js/tools/world-client.js's own loadRegion() parser, NOT the native-render Rust crate's raw XTEA-decoded format which is a different schema/source entirely) — region base is `((x>>6)<<8)|(y>>6)` world tile, `(rid>>8)*64,(rid&255)*64`. Cross-referencing an object/npc's wiki-cited coordinate against the matching region file (same id occurring naturally nearby) is a strong, fast, fully-offline corroboration step before authoring a new native scenario — caught 6 bad candidates (2 stale mapMarkers, 2 non-static-spawn NPCs, 1 wiki/row region mismatch, 1 plane-1-only NPC) before any capture was attempted.
[media-capture] native NPC models decode HEAD/crown-ONLY (no body/torso) for human NPCs in the current renderer — a floating head reads as a dropped item, not a standing figure (confirmed eye-verify: King Roald 5215 = floating gold crown, Dimintheis 4984/Curator 5214 = floating head + black artifacts, Orlando 6001/Warrior 1158 = head/hat blob). This is a KNOWN, already-accepted native limitation (PARITY_REPORT.md line 255 documents the shipped train-thieving-20 capture as "player + 2 heads"), NOT a framing/coords fault. CONSEQUENCE for media-capture batches: a single-NPC quest-start scene captured natively reads poorly until the NPC-body-composition fix lands — OBJECT/scenery-anchored subjects (rocks/trees/anvils/fires/altars) render as FULL models and are strongly preferred; hold NPC-only quest-start captures for the body-composition fix rather than wiring floating heads. Coordinator held 5 grounded+correctly-framed NPC scenarios (priest-in-peril/hunter-9/lost-city/family-crest/bone-voyage) on exactly this basis for a separate native-render fix to re-capture.
[media-capture] default native camera framing rule (no engine change needed): the eye sits at (px+2,py-2) looking NW at the player, so a subject only renders LARGE + readable when placed in the SE FOREGROUND toward the eye. Proven-clean geometry = subject at player+(+1,0) (due east, beside player, e.g. willow/anvil) or player+(0,-1) (due south, e.g. agility wall); a big object also works at player+(+1,-1) (barbarian fire). AVOID: subject NE/N of the player (background -> shrinks to a tiny upper-left blob) and subject ON the eye tile player+(+2,-2) (out of frustum -> does not render at all, the original train-mining-30 miss). For a single NPC use player+(+1,0) so it stands BESIDE the player, not player+(+1,-1) which stacks it dead-center on the eye->player sightline and occludes the player. To keep a subject at its true cache coord, move the PLAYER instead: player = subject+(-1,0).
